  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study constructs a Multidimensional Housing Quality Index (MHQI) using household-level microdata from Ghana's 2021 Population and Housing Census (N = 836,515) to evaluate housing deprivation across nine indicators of structure, space, services, and sanitation. Applying principal component analysis to a tetrachoric correlation matrix, the index reveals a national mean score of 62.2 out of 100, with urban households scoring 73.1 compared to 45.3 for rural households. The Alkire–Foster framework classifies 56.5% of households as housing poor, with district-level scores showing strong spatial clustering (Moran's I > 0.71).",
      "why": "The research provides a transferable, empirical framework to measure multidimensional housing deprivation in the Global South, shifting the policy focus from broad national deficits to targeted, district-level interventions addressing specific local infrastructure gaps."
    }
